1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:05,340
The next of the normalcytic endemias are those with predominantly intravascular hemolysis,

2
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:09,780
and the first of them that we want to cover is paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.

3
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:13,720
I actually prefer to explain this in my own words, and then I'll walk you through the slides,

4
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:17,940
so let's just take a blank screen for a second and remind ourselves of a couple points.

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,480
First of all, the red blood cells, they live in a very dangerous environment.

6
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:24,160
They live right next to complement. Their neighbors are deadly.

7
00:00:24,740 --> 00:00:28,680
And so the red blood cell needs to be protected against complement,

8
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:31,040
and in fact, this doesn't apply to the red blood cell only.

9
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:34,400
It also applies to the platelets and the white cells as well.

10
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:40,600
And so the red blood cells, in order to protect themselves against being destroyed by their neighbor, i.e. complement,

11
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:47,180
they actually have a molecule on their surface, and that molecule on their surface is called DAF, decay accelerating factor.

12
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:53,900
They also have another molecule on their surface, which is called MERL, membrane inhibitor of reactive lysis.

13
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:57,980
And so these two molecules, what they do is work complement to get activated.

14
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:03,720
If complement were to try to come anywhere near the red blood cell, these two molecules would inactivate the complement.

15
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:11,240
DAF stands for decay accelerating factor, and it accelerates the decay of C3 convertase, for example.

16
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,760
Now, it's important to then note that DAF and MERL are both connected to the red blood cell membrane by an anchoring protein called GPI.

17
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:23,200
So this is GPI.

18
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:29,380
And this anchoring protein is essential because it's what allows DAF and MERL to be expressed on the surface of the red blood cell.

19
00:01:29,980 --> 00:01:34,220
In paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, the problem is the patients get an acquired.

20
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:35,420
Note that it's an acquired.

21
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:36,280
It's not inherited.

22
00:01:36,540 --> 00:01:37,760
It occurs later in life.

23
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:40,940
It's an acquired defect in the myeloid stem cell.

24
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:48,680
And because there is a defect in the myeloid stem cell, GPI will not be present in all cells derived from the myeloid lineage.

25
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,280
So, for example, the platelets will not have GPI.

26
00:01:52,660 --> 00:01:54,360
The red blood cells will not have GPI.

27
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:58,540
And the white blood cells, in particular the granulocytes, will not have GPI.

28
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:02,840
And because there is no GPI, there's going to be no DAF.

29
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:10,660
And if there is no DAF, and there is no MERL, for example, then the cells will be susceptible to destruction by complement.

30
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:14,020
And that's the problem in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.

31
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:17,540
And that is that the cells are susceptible to complement destruction.

32
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:19,020
Now, we're not done there.

33
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:23,500
We've got to go one more step further and explain the name, paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.

34
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:28,460
In order to understand this, you have to go back and remind yourselves from physiology,

35
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,920
when we sleep at night, we actually breathe shallow.

36
00:02:31,920 --> 00:02:35,720
And when our breathing becomes shallow, we retain carbon dioxide.

37
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:41,380
And when we retain carbon dioxide, that then gives us a respiratory acidosis.

38
00:02:42,020 --> 00:02:44,500
Now, acidosis has the ability to activate complement.

39
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:47,200
So, at night, we are activating a little bit of complement.

40
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:53,660
Of course, patients who have normal red blood cells and normal white blood cells and normal platelets

41
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,460
would have no problem with this activation.

42
00:02:55,640 --> 00:02:58,060
They could simply protect themselves against that activation.

43
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:03,120
However, in patients who have an absent GPI and don't have these protective molecules,

44
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:09,100
they will be susceptible to complement mediated damage in particular or classically when they're sleeping at night.

45
00:03:09,580 --> 00:03:14,720
And so that's why it's called paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria because on some nights, i.e. paroxysmal,

46
00:03:15,580 --> 00:03:21,480
when the patients are sleeping, they actually get lysis of their red cells and platelets and granulocytes,

47
00:03:21,480 --> 00:03:25,360
but in particular, they're red cells, which then results in the hemoglobinuria.

48
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:28,340
When they wake up in the morning, they've got very dark urine.

49
00:03:28,760 --> 00:03:29,560
So that's the summary.

50
00:03:29,700 --> 00:03:31,720
Now, let me walk you through what the text says.

51
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:35,880
Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria is an acquired defect.

52
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:36,700
That's important.

53
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:39,380
In the myeloid stem cell, that's important.

54
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:41,640
Results in absent GPI.

55
00:03:41,640 --> 00:03:48,540
This then renders the cell susceptible to complement because there is no DAF and there is no MERL present on their cell surface.

56
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:53,820
In particular, the patients get intravascular hemolysis that occurs at night.

57
00:03:54,380 --> 00:04:01,260
Intravascular because the complement destroys the red blood cell within the blood vessel at night because that's when complement gets activated.

58
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:03,760
Why does it get activated at night?

59
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:10,620
Because there's shallow breathing resulting in a respiratory acidosis, which then results in activation of complement.

60
00:04:10,620 --> 00:04:19,700
And, of course, the red blood cells, the granulocytes, and the platelets are all lysed because each of these cells are lacking the GPI linker molecule.

61
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:24,680
This presents with intravascular hemolysis, so the patients present with hemoglobinemia.

62
00:04:24,940 --> 00:04:27,000
If you were to look in their blood, you would see hemoglobin.

63
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:31,260
Some of that hemoglobin will leak out into the urine, giving you hemoglobinuria.

64
00:04:31,740 --> 00:04:37,560
And also, some of the hemoglobin in the urine will be taken up by the tubular cells in the kidney.

65
00:04:37,560 --> 00:04:42,820
And as it piles up in the tubular cells of the kidney, it'll pile up as hemocytorin.

66
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:49,520
Now, eventually, the tubular cells of the kidney will be sloughed off as they turn over, and that'll give us hemocytorinuria.

67
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:52,420
But that will be seen days after the hemolysis.

68
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:54,300
So it won't be seen with the acute episode.

69
00:04:54,660 --> 00:05:00,340
But it might be useful if you're suspecting hemolysis and you want to check for it days after the event occurred.

70
00:05:00,340 --> 00:05:05,680
One way by which we can screen for this disease is to introduce sucrose into the patient's serum.

71
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:11,700
When we introduce sucrose, that activates complement, and that could then destroy the red blood cells in a screening test.

72
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:14,780
A confirmatory test would be to acidify the serum.

73
00:05:14,900 --> 00:05:20,180
If you acidify the serum, you would activate complement, which would then also confirm the disease.

74
00:05:20,180 --> 00:05:30,080
Nowadays, the more recent test and the test that's more often used in laboratories today is to look for a lack of CD55 on the surface of the red blood cells.

75
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:39,260
If you have red blood cells and you want to see whether they have CD55, an important thing about CD55 is that it uses GPI as a linker molecule.

76
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:49,060
So if there is a population of red cells in the patient that lacks CD55, that would be a way by which you could prove that the patient has paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobin area.

77
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:55,280
And CD55 really is DAF, so that makes sense because DAF was the whole molecule that we started this discussion with.

78
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:58,600
The main cause of death in patients is thrombosis.

79
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:04,760
The idea here is that the complement is going to destroy the red cells, the platelets, and the granulocytes.

80
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:11,540
And so when it destroys the platelets, the fragments of the destroyed platelets can actually go into the circulation,

81
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:17,060
and that can activate the coagulation cascade, resulting in thrombosis.

82
00:06:17,520 --> 00:06:19,120
And so that's the main cause of death.

83
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:22,660
And so examiners love to go after it because it really tests you on two principles.

84
00:06:22,820 --> 00:06:25,760
One, you need to remember that the platelets are also lacking GPI.

85
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:34,740
And two, that's the major cause of death in these patients, thrombosis of hepatic vein or portal vein or cerebral veins, for example.

86
00:06:35,720 --> 00:06:38,260
Complications of the disease include iron deficiency anemia.

87
00:06:38,480 --> 00:06:40,140
Why would they get iron deficiency anemia?

88
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:44,420
The answer is these patients are losing iron in their urine.

89
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:47,420
As they're losing hemoglobin, they're actually losing iron.

90
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:50,000
And so they can develop an iron deficiency anemia.

91
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:53,140
In addition, 10% of the patients can develop AML.

92
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:58,780
Now remember that I told you that this disease is a mutation in the myeloid stem cell.

93
00:06:58,780 --> 00:07:04,860
So imagine if there's one mutation in the myeloid stem cell, it's not going to be hard to develop another mutation in that cell,

94
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:07,060
and eventually that could result in AML.

95
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:12,700
And 10% of the patients do develop AML, and that is a high-yield association as well.

96
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:21,020
The second of the predominantly intravascular hemolysis disorders is G6PD deficiency.

97
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:26,000
G6PD deficiency, again, requires a little bit of biochemistry to understand,

98
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:30,440
so what I prefer to do is explain it in my own way, and then I'll walk you through the slides.

99
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:35,740
When you think about G6PD deficiency, what you need to understand is that the red blood cells,

100
00:07:35,960 --> 00:07:40,200
they live in an environment of oxidative stress.

101
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:44,060
The red blood cells, they live in an environment of oxidative stress.

102
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:49,520
To protect themselves against that oxidative stress, for example, just by the fact that they carry oxygen,

103
00:07:49,720 --> 00:07:55,060
by the fact that there's going to be inflammation in the body, etc., those are all oxidative stresses.

104
00:07:55,660 --> 00:08:01,140
To protect themselves against oxidative stress, they use a special molecule called glutathione.

105
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:04,780
So glutathione is very important to protect against oxidative stress.

106
00:08:04,780 --> 00:08:14,020
For example, H2O2, which is one of the major sources of oxidative stress, can be taken by glutathione,

107
00:08:14,620 --> 00:08:27,990
and it can be reduced such that glutathione will reduce it and then become oxidized itself, resulting in GSSG.

108
00:08:27,990 --> 00:08:36,910
Now, when glutathione gets oxidized and takes the hit to protect the cell against H2O2,

109
00:08:37,450 --> 00:08:40,910
that's great because it protects it against an oxidative stress.

110
00:08:41,210 --> 00:08:44,530
However, in order to continue to protect the cell against oxidative stress,

111
00:08:44,850 --> 00:08:48,610
it needs to be reduced back into glutathione.

112
00:08:49,170 --> 00:08:55,030
The molecule that's necessary to do this is NADPH.

113
00:08:55,030 --> 00:09:03,990
And NADPH is produced via G6PD, glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase.

114
00:09:04,070 --> 00:09:08,670
So one of the byproducts of glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase is NADPH.

115
00:09:09,310 --> 00:09:15,950
No glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase, i.e., glucose 6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, means no NADPH.

116
00:09:16,570 --> 00:09:20,430
No NADPH means no ability to regenerate reduced glutathione.

117
00:09:20,430 --> 00:09:27,610
No ability to regenerate reduced glutathione means that there's going to be increased oxidative damage to the red blood cells.

118
00:09:28,250 --> 00:09:31,390
So that is G6PD deficiency in a nutshell.

119
00:09:31,650 --> 00:09:32,890
Now let's walk through the slides.

120
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:38,790
So in G6PD deficiency, it's an X-linked recessive disorder with a reduced half-life.

121
00:09:38,870 --> 00:09:40,290
That's important, reduced half-life.

122
00:09:40,370 --> 00:09:41,570
I'll highlight that in a moment.

123
00:09:42,030 --> 00:09:44,950
This renders the cells susceptible to oxidative stress.

124
00:09:45,530 --> 00:09:47,350
Now G6PD has two major variants.

125
00:09:47,350 --> 00:09:54,530
There's an African variant where there's a mildly reduced half-life, and there's a Mediterranean variant where there's a markedly reduced half-life.

126
00:09:55,090 --> 00:10:01,270
The high gene frequency in both of these populations is because it has a protective role against falciparum, malaria.

127
00:10:02,850 --> 00:10:07,410
Let me go back and just remind you that when we talk about the two variants,

128
00:10:07,790 --> 00:10:12,810
the two variants can result in somewhat different clinical severity of disease.

129
00:10:12,810 --> 00:10:17,390
So remember that in the African variant, there is a mildly reduced half-life of G6PD.

130
00:10:17,550 --> 00:10:18,670
So let's just draw a few cells.

131
00:10:18,770 --> 00:10:24,590
Let's pretend this is 30 days old, 60 days old, 90 days old, and 120 days old.

132
00:10:25,030 --> 00:10:29,710
So 30, 60, 90, and 120.

133
00:10:30,350 --> 00:10:32,010
And let's draw this same thing here.

134
00:10:32,150 --> 00:10:35,210
30, 60, 90, and 120.

135
00:10:35,210 --> 00:10:44,210
Now in the mild form, well, why don't we take a step back?

136
00:10:44,350 --> 00:10:50,210
In the normal patient, G6PD's half-life is such that it lasts the entire life of the red blood cell,

137
00:10:50,450 --> 00:10:54,450
so that a normal red blood cell, no matter how old it is, has sufficient G6PD.

138
00:10:54,450 --> 00:11:02,930
In the mildly reduced half-life, the G6PD is not present in cells that are older because, obviously, they've lived longer,

139
00:11:03,090 --> 00:11:08,650
and the half-life of the enzyme was not able to allow the enzyme to be present for a long period of time.

140
00:11:08,990 --> 00:11:13,530
So in a mildly reduced half-life, maybe only the older cells don't have G6PD.

141
00:11:14,030 --> 00:11:19,390
So if there's an oxidative stress, then what will happen is only the cells that are older will be destroyed,

142
00:11:19,890 --> 00:11:21,390
and that's the African variant.

143
00:11:21,390 --> 00:11:28,190
In the Mediterranean variant, where there's a markedly reduced half-life, the G6PD is removed much earlier.

144
00:11:28,390 --> 00:11:36,590
And so, for example, when there's an oxidative stress, all these cells at greater than the age of 30 days, for example, will die.

145
00:11:36,950 --> 00:11:40,590
And that's because there is a markedly reduced half-life of G6PD.

146
00:11:41,390 --> 00:11:45,210
Now, what are the oxidative stresses to be aware of?

147
00:11:45,210 --> 00:11:51,310
Well, the causes of oxidative stress include infections, drugs, and fava beans.

148
00:11:51,830 --> 00:11:57,990
And fava beans are classic, especially in the Mediterranean variant, because they are particularly consumed in the Mediterranean regions.

149
00:11:58,530 --> 00:12:04,910
When the oxidative stress hits the cell, then that causes hemoglobin to precipitate as a Heinz body.

150
00:12:05,390 --> 00:12:10,650
The precipitate of hemoglobin is then removed by the splenic macrophages, resulting in bite cells.

151
00:12:10,650 --> 00:12:14,490
And that then results in intravascular hemolysis.

152
00:12:14,850 --> 00:12:21,690
Now, there may be some extravascular hemolysis, in that the macrophages may sometimes consume the entire cell, rather than just creating a bite.

153
00:12:21,990 --> 00:12:25,970
But the predominant hemolysis is intravascular.

154
00:12:26,570 --> 00:12:28,350
Here's an example of a bite cell.

155
00:12:28,610 --> 00:12:32,270
The splenic macrophage has taken a bite out of this cell.

156
00:12:32,270 --> 00:12:38,210
And the idea here is that if there is a big precipitative hemoglobin, the splenic macrophage will simply remove that.

157
00:12:38,330 --> 00:12:42,130
And when it removes it, it's going to end up removing a big piece of the cell with it.

158
00:12:42,870 --> 00:12:45,950
Now, these patients are then going to get intravascular hemolysis.

159
00:12:46,430 --> 00:12:48,090
This presents with hemoglobinuria.

160
00:12:48,330 --> 00:12:51,510
So they get hemoglobin in the urine because of intravascular hemolysis.

161
00:12:51,850 --> 00:12:55,470
And classically get back pain because the hemoglobin is nephrotoxic.

162
00:12:55,710 --> 00:12:58,550
And so that results in the back pain that's classically seen.

163
00:12:58,550 --> 00:13:02,590
This occurs hours after exposure to an oxidative stress.

164
00:13:02,950 --> 00:13:08,330
If you want to try to see the precipitated hemoglobin, you won't be able to see it in the normal blood smear.

165
00:13:08,790 --> 00:13:13,550
Instead, a special preparation called the Heinz preparation must be made.

166
00:13:13,950 --> 00:13:16,190
And this will be a screen for the disease.

167
00:13:16,650 --> 00:13:20,710
The Heinz preparation will then highlight the precipitated hemoglobin.

168
00:13:20,850 --> 00:13:25,910
And so here's an example of a Heinz prep showing the aggregate of hemoglobin.

169
00:13:25,910 --> 00:13:30,450
Again, important to note that if you want to confirm the disease, you must do enzymatic studies.

170
00:13:30,810 --> 00:13:36,810
But you will not do the enzymatic studies until after the disease has resolved, i.e. after the acute episode.

171
00:13:37,310 --> 00:13:43,370
If you did the enzymatic studies at the time that there was hemolysis, all the cells that are lacking the enzyme will be dead.

172
00:13:43,670 --> 00:13:50,830
And when you test the remaining red cells, those are the ones that actually have the enzyme because they had a half-life sufficient within that red blood cell.

173
00:13:50,830 --> 00:13:57,090
So we don't do the enzyme test until the patient has actually resolved the acute hemolytic episode.

174
00:13:57,550 --> 00:13:59,630
And that's an important take-home point as well.

175
00:14:00,050 --> 00:14:05,210
Another important cause of hemolysis is immune hemolytic anemia.

176
00:14:06,010 --> 00:14:10,510
Now, immune hemolytic anemia is antibody-mediated destruction of the red blood cells.

177
00:14:10,630 --> 00:14:12,990
And it can be due to IgG or IgM.

178
00:14:12,990 --> 00:14:21,650
If it's due to IgG, that's called IgG-mediated disease, and that usually is extravascular hemolysis.

179
00:14:22,050 --> 00:14:25,190
The idea here is that the IgG will bind the red blood cell.

180
00:14:25,490 --> 00:14:31,550
It's also called a warm agglutinin because it binds the red blood cell in the relatively warm temperature of the central body.

181
00:14:31,550 --> 00:14:40,330
When the RBC is coated with the IgG, the membrane and IgG will actually be eaten up by the splenic macrophages,

182
00:14:40,550 --> 00:14:43,330
and that will slowly remove membrane from the cell.

183
00:14:43,690 --> 00:14:47,510
And the slow loss of membrane from the cell will result in spherocytes.

184
00:14:47,610 --> 00:14:50,270
It's very similar to hereditary spherocytosis.

185
00:14:50,270 --> 00:14:57,730
Again, the idea is that if this is the red blood cell, and you slowly pick off a little bit of membrane using an antibody,

186
00:14:58,190 --> 00:15:04,090
then as you take away more and more membrane, you're going to lose excess membrane, and these cells will convert to spherocytes.

187
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:09,950
So on blood smear, you would see the same thing that you would see in hereditary spherocytosis.

188
00:15:10,830 --> 00:15:15,190
This IgG-mediated disease is associated with lupus.

189
00:15:15,190 --> 00:15:21,530
And remember that in lupus, you can get antibodies against red cells, antibodies against platelets, or antibodies against white cells.

190
00:15:22,070 --> 00:15:23,910
It's also associated with CLL.

191
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:31,390
In chronic lymphocytic leukemia, one of the complications is that the patients can actually develop an autoimmune hemolytic anemia, usually IgG,

192
00:15:31,870 --> 00:15:34,010
and it's also associated with certain drugs.

193
00:15:34,530 --> 00:15:39,550
Now, when it's associated with drugs, there's a couple ways by which those antibodies can actually cause destruction.

194
00:15:40,070 --> 00:15:43,610
One way is that the drug can actually attach to the red blood cell membrane,

195
00:15:43,610 --> 00:15:50,210
and that occurs, for example, with penicillin, and then the antibody will bind the drug membrane complex,

196
00:15:50,570 --> 00:15:54,570
and that will then allow for removal of pieces of membrane.

197
00:15:55,070 --> 00:16:00,810
Another way is that the drug can actually induce production of autoantibodies itself,

198
00:16:00,950 --> 00:16:03,570
i.e. the drug causes the production of autoantibodies.

199
00:16:04,050 --> 00:16:06,090
An example of that would be methyl dopa,

200
00:16:06,090 --> 00:16:11,790
and those antibodies then bind antigens that are present already on the red blood cell.

201
00:16:12,130 --> 00:16:17,110
So that's a couple mechanisms of how drugs might result in autoimmune hemolytic anemia.

202
00:16:17,770 --> 00:16:21,850
The treatment is going to be to remove the drug, if it's indeed caused by a drug.

203
00:16:22,490 --> 00:16:25,310
Steroids, to slow down the production of the antibody.

204
00:16:25,310 --> 00:16:31,990
I.V.I.G., this is IV immunoglobulin, and the idea here is that if you give IV immunoglobulin,

205
00:16:32,270 --> 00:16:39,030
the splenic macrophages will eat the IV immunoglobulin instead of eating the immunoglobulin attached to the red blood cells.

206
00:16:39,590 --> 00:16:43,670
And so IV.I.G. is like throwing a stick at a dog so that it runs the other way.

207
00:16:43,830 --> 00:16:48,430
You throw a stick at the spleen, so it starts eating the IV.I.G. instead of the red blood cells.

208
00:16:48,430 --> 00:16:49,830
It buys you some time.

209
00:16:50,770 --> 00:16:54,430
And then splenectomy could be performed because there would be two benefits.

210
00:16:54,570 --> 00:16:56,970
Number one, it removes the source of antibody.

211
00:16:58,010 --> 00:17:06,830
And number two, it removes the source of destruction because it's predominantly the spleen that's eating those antibody-bound red blood cells.

212
00:17:07,450 --> 00:17:10,170
And so that's a little bit about treatment.

213
00:17:10,330 --> 00:17:12,270
Of course, splenectomy is the last resort.

214
00:17:13,590 --> 00:17:17,430
I.G.M.-mediated disease usually involves intravascular hemolysis,

215
00:17:17,430 --> 00:17:23,410
and the idea here is that I.G.M. binds RBCs, usually in the colder temperature of the extremities,

216
00:17:23,490 --> 00:17:24,930
for example, the fingers and the toes.

217
00:17:25,410 --> 00:17:31,990
It then fixes complement, and when it fixes complement, that results in formation of a membrane attack complex,

218
00:17:32,430 --> 00:17:34,810
which then results in an intravascular hemolysis.

219
00:17:35,290 --> 00:17:39,250
Now, this is associated with two very high-yield concepts.

220
00:17:39,370 --> 00:17:42,110
Number one, mycoplasma pneumoniae.

221
00:17:42,110 --> 00:17:48,090
Patients with mycoplasma pneumoniae can subsequently develop an I.G.M.-mediated intravascular hemolysis,

222
00:17:48,090 --> 00:17:53,370
which would be autoimmune, and also it can be seen with infectious mononucleosis.

223
00:17:53,850 --> 00:18:00,350
So those are a couple of high-yield associations for I.G.M.-mediated autoimmune destruction of red blood cells.

224
00:18:01,250 --> 00:18:05,230
The Coombs test is what is used to diagnose immune hemolytic anemia.

225
00:18:05,230 --> 00:18:06,950
There are two types of Coombs test.

226
00:18:07,090 --> 00:18:08,410
There's a direct and an indirect.

227
00:18:08,970 --> 00:18:12,210
The idea in the direct Coombs test is that you're asking the question.

228
00:18:12,290 --> 00:18:14,570
Remember, whenever you do a test, you're asking a question.

229
00:18:14,750 --> 00:18:16,350
And so what's the question in this test?

230
00:18:16,730 --> 00:18:22,610
The question is, do I have red blood cells that are already bound by I.G.G.?

231
00:18:22,610 --> 00:18:26,290
So you've got these red blood cells, and they're already bound by I.G.G.,

232
00:18:26,290 --> 00:18:28,150
and you're asking the question, are these present?

233
00:18:28,150 --> 00:18:32,190
So what you do is you introduce another antibody against I.G.G.,

234
00:18:32,190 --> 00:18:36,130
and if this exists, then that will cause agglutination.

235
00:18:36,290 --> 00:18:39,750
It will cause the red cells to pile up and precipitate out.

236
00:18:39,990 --> 00:18:41,990
So that's the direct Coombs test.

237
00:18:42,070 --> 00:18:45,430
You take anti-I.G.G., you add it to the patient's R.B.C.s.

238
00:18:45,690 --> 00:18:50,870
You assume, or you're asking the question, are the R.B.C.s already coded with I.G.G.?

239
00:18:50,870 --> 00:18:52,990
And if they are, agglutination will occur.

240
00:18:53,530 --> 00:18:56,510
This is the most important test for immune hemolytic anemia.

241
00:18:57,110 --> 00:18:59,330
The indirect Coombs test asks a different question.

242
00:18:59,450 --> 00:19:03,210
It asks the question, does the patient have antibodies in their serum?

243
00:19:03,950 --> 00:19:08,750
So now what you're doing is you're taking R.B.C.s from, you know, these are test R.B.C.s,

244
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:13,110
and you're putting in the patient's serum and asking the question,

245
00:19:13,270 --> 00:19:15,070
are there these antibodies in the serum?

246
00:19:15,250 --> 00:19:17,170
If they are, they'll bind the R.B.C.s,

247
00:19:17,170 --> 00:19:19,910
and you're going to add an anti-I.G.G. antibody,

248
00:19:20,290 --> 00:19:21,950
which will then result in agglutination.

249
00:19:22,550 --> 00:19:24,650
So the direct test asks the question,

250
00:19:24,650 --> 00:19:28,510
do I have red blood cells that are already coded with antibody?

251
00:19:28,930 --> 00:19:30,930
And the indirect test asks the question,

252
00:19:31,310 --> 00:19:34,290
does the patient's serum have the antibody?

253
00:19:34,590 --> 00:19:37,070
So that's important to understand that distinction.

254
00:19:38,130 --> 00:19:40,890
Another way by which we can get intravascular hemolysis

255
00:19:40,890 --> 00:19:43,490
is called microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

256
00:19:44,350 --> 00:19:47,110
Microangio, small blood vessel, pathic pathology.

257
00:19:47,490 --> 00:19:49,790
So there is a pathology in the small blood vessel

258
00:19:49,790 --> 00:19:51,750
that results in a hemolytic anemia.

259
00:19:52,270 --> 00:19:55,750
Now the idea here is that you've got a small blood vessel,

260
00:19:56,110 --> 00:19:59,590
and of course this blood vessel is lined by endothelial cells.

261
00:20:00,350 --> 00:20:01,910
And what happens in this case

262
00:20:01,910 --> 00:20:03,870
is that you get some sort of thrombus.

263
00:20:04,370 --> 00:20:05,970
It could be a platelet thrombus.

264
00:20:06,070 --> 00:20:08,770
It could be a platelet plus fibrin thrombus,

265
00:20:08,950 --> 00:20:11,330
which blocks the blood vessel partially

266
00:20:11,330 --> 00:20:14,190
so that when the red blood cells go across this blood vessel,

267
00:20:14,350 --> 00:20:15,270
they get sheared,

268
00:20:15,430 --> 00:20:17,410
and they develop into cells that look like this,

269
00:20:17,410 --> 00:20:19,330
which are called schistocytes.

270
00:20:20,110 --> 00:20:21,590
And schistocytes are the hallmark

271
00:20:21,590 --> 00:20:23,730
of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.

272
00:20:24,390 --> 00:20:26,470
Now microangiopathic hemolytic anemia

273
00:20:26,470 --> 00:20:29,170
can be seen in a few circumstances.

274
00:20:29,950 --> 00:20:31,330
The first would be TTP,

275
00:20:31,570 --> 00:20:33,770
thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura.

276
00:20:34,250 --> 00:20:35,390
And in this particular case,

277
00:20:35,490 --> 00:20:37,370
you get platelet thrombi,

278
00:20:37,570 --> 00:20:38,390
platelet thrombi,

279
00:20:38,850 --> 00:20:40,290
and it's due to the fact

280
00:20:40,290 --> 00:20:43,610
that the patient lacks an enzyme called ADAMS-TS13.

281
00:20:43,610 --> 00:20:45,610
We've discussed this in detail

282
00:20:45,610 --> 00:20:48,010
in our coagulation lecture,

283
00:20:48,170 --> 00:20:49,830
so I won't repeat it here,

284
00:20:49,950 --> 00:20:51,930
but you can refer back there if you need to.

285
00:20:52,570 --> 00:20:55,270
In HUS, which is sort of the sister disease,

286
00:20:55,390 --> 00:20:57,330
you also have platelet microthrombi,

287
00:20:57,590 --> 00:21:00,010
but in hemolytic uremic syndrome, i.e. HUS,

288
00:21:00,470 --> 00:21:05,250
it is due to a toxin produced by E. coli 015787.

289
00:21:05,670 --> 00:21:09,030
Again, we've also covered this in our previous discussions.

290
00:21:09,030 --> 00:21:13,430
In DIC, you get platelet plus fibrin thrombi,

291
00:21:13,630 --> 00:21:15,030
and the platelet fibrin thrombi

292
00:21:15,030 --> 00:21:16,910
would then again result in schistocytes.

293
00:21:17,430 --> 00:21:18,790
We've covered that previously.

294
00:21:19,330 --> 00:21:21,570
And in a fourth scenario called HELP,

295
00:21:22,310 --> 00:21:26,570
hemolysis elevated liver enzymes and low platelets.

296
00:21:27,090 --> 00:21:28,830
A pregnant woman can develop

297
00:21:28,830 --> 00:21:31,630
the microangiopathic hemolytic anemia

298
00:21:31,630 --> 00:21:32,610
within the liver

299
00:21:32,610 --> 00:21:35,010
due to the production of these thrombi as well.

300
00:21:35,450 --> 00:21:37,090
And we'll discuss that in more detail

301
00:21:37,090 --> 00:21:39,690
when we do the gynecologic lectures.

302
00:21:40,050 --> 00:21:41,450
But the idea here is that

303
00:21:41,450 --> 00:21:43,290
you've got a small vessel pathology

304
00:21:43,290 --> 00:21:46,010
that results in production of schistocytes

305
00:21:46,010 --> 00:21:48,050
and a hemolytic anemia.

306
00:21:48,870 --> 00:21:51,210
We can also get loss of red blood cell

307
00:21:51,210 --> 00:21:53,650
within the vasculature with prostatic heart valves,

308
00:21:53,950 --> 00:21:56,270
and that's because the valves crush the red blood cells,

309
00:21:56,350 --> 00:21:57,050
destroying them,

310
00:21:57,350 --> 00:21:58,750
and with aortic stenosis.

311
00:21:59,390 --> 00:22:01,510
The calcified degenerative valve

312
00:22:01,510 --> 00:22:04,050
will also again crush the red blood cells

313
00:22:04,050 --> 00:22:04,970
and destroy them.

314
00:22:04,970 --> 00:22:06,370
So these are some circumstances

315
00:22:06,370 --> 00:22:07,730
in which patients might get

316
00:22:07,730 --> 00:22:10,550
a hemolytic anemia inside the blood vessel.

317
00:22:11,250 --> 00:22:12,510
Again, I've already told you

318
00:22:12,510 --> 00:22:15,390
that microthrombi produce schistocytes on blood smears.

319
00:22:15,510 --> 00:22:18,070
So whenever you have a microangiopathic hemolytic anemia,

320
00:22:18,250 --> 00:22:19,590
you think about schistocytes.

321
00:22:19,990 --> 00:22:22,050
And this is the classic image of schistocytes

322
00:22:22,050 --> 00:22:24,690
that I've already presented in a prior lecture.

323
00:22:24,810 --> 00:22:26,330
Notice that it looks like a helmet,

324
00:22:26,530 --> 00:22:27,970
and that's the classic finding

325
00:22:27,970 --> 00:22:30,170
is that you have points at either end of the cell

326
00:22:30,170 --> 00:22:33,050
with a shearing of the cell across the cytoplasm.

327
00:22:33,050 --> 00:22:36,330
Another way by which red blood cells can be destroyed

328
00:22:36,330 --> 00:22:37,330
is malaria.

329
00:22:37,830 --> 00:22:40,470
Malaria results in infection of the red blood cells

330
00:22:40,470 --> 00:22:42,470
and the liver with plasmodium,

331
00:22:42,850 --> 00:22:45,730
and it's transmitted by the anophilus mosquito,

332
00:22:46,090 --> 00:22:47,570
and that's important to know as well.

333
00:22:47,970 --> 00:22:49,250
This is the classic finding.

334
00:22:49,690 --> 00:22:52,050
We will see little fragments of,

335
00:22:52,130 --> 00:22:53,230
or little organisms,

336
00:22:53,410 --> 00:22:56,930
malarial organisms present within the red blood cells.

337
00:22:57,170 --> 00:22:58,310
It's important to note

338
00:22:58,310 --> 00:22:59,730
that what causes the hemolysis

339
00:22:59,730 --> 00:23:03,650
is that the RBCs rupture as part of the plasmodium life cycle,

340
00:23:04,070 --> 00:23:07,250
so that each time the plasmodium undergoes its life cycle

341
00:23:07,250 --> 00:23:08,250
and begins to reproduce,

342
00:23:08,710 --> 00:23:10,550
eventually it'll cause rupture of the cell.

343
00:23:10,990 --> 00:23:13,350
What's interesting is that the rupture of the cell

344
00:23:13,350 --> 00:23:14,410
will result in fever,

345
00:23:14,850 --> 00:23:16,090
so that, for example,

346
00:23:16,270 --> 00:23:18,570
with plasmodium falciparum,

347
00:23:18,850 --> 00:23:20,170
the patients get daily fever,

348
00:23:20,510 --> 00:23:22,350
whereas with vivax and ovale,

349
00:23:22,670 --> 00:23:24,270
they get fever every other day.

350
00:23:24,270 --> 00:23:26,190
So, again, just highlighting,

351
00:23:26,410 --> 00:23:29,050
just allowing you to remember that in malaria,

352
00:23:29,270 --> 00:23:30,170
patients would also get

353
00:23:30,170 --> 00:23:32,830
a predominantly intravascular hemolysis.

354
00:23:33,330 --> 00:23:35,070
However, the spleen will also consume

355
00:23:35,070 --> 00:23:36,610
some of the infected red blood cells,

356
00:23:36,950 --> 00:23:39,530
and so there will be some extravascular hemolysis

357
00:23:39,530 --> 00:23:40,990
with splenomegaly.

358
00:23:40,990 --> 00:23:44,090
The final discussion that we want to have

359
00:23:44,090 --> 00:23:47,130
is anemias due to underproduction,

360
00:23:47,610 --> 00:23:48,990
and the idea here, again,

361
00:23:49,070 --> 00:23:50,570
is that you get decreased production

362
00:23:50,570 --> 00:23:52,450
of the red blood cells by the bone marrow,

363
00:23:52,590 --> 00:23:53,350
and it's characterized

364
00:23:53,350 --> 00:23:55,990
by a low corrected reticulocyte count.

365
00:23:56,690 --> 00:23:59,570
The etiologies of underproduction bone marrow include

366
00:23:59,570 --> 00:24:02,130
anything that causes a microcytic anemia

367
00:24:02,130 --> 00:24:03,630
or a macrocytic anemia

368
00:24:03,630 --> 00:24:06,010
will usually have a low reticulocyte count,

369
00:24:06,150 --> 00:24:06,710
so, for example,

370
00:24:06,830 --> 00:24:08,110
an iron deficiency anemia,

371
00:24:08,250 --> 00:24:10,510
the reticulocyte count tends to be low,

372
00:24:10,510 --> 00:24:13,430
in vitamin B12 and folate deficiency anemia,

373
00:24:13,870 --> 00:24:15,890
the reticulocyte count tends to be low.

374
00:24:16,450 --> 00:24:18,430
Renal failure will also result

375
00:24:18,430 --> 00:24:20,010
in low production by the bone marrow

376
00:24:20,010 --> 00:24:22,830
because there will be less erythropoietin produced,

377
00:24:23,330 --> 00:24:24,610
reticulocyte count will be low,

378
00:24:24,970 --> 00:24:25,890
and if there's damage

379
00:24:25,890 --> 00:24:27,830
to the bone marrow precursor cells,

380
00:24:28,270 --> 00:24:29,450
that would also result

381
00:24:29,450 --> 00:24:31,670
in a low reticulocyte count

382
00:24:31,670 --> 00:24:33,470
because the progenitor cells

383
00:24:33,470 --> 00:24:34,670
are not functioning properly.

384
00:24:35,170 --> 00:24:37,830
One classic example would be parvovirus B19.

385
00:24:38,350 --> 00:24:40,230
We've talked about it several times already,

386
00:24:40,510 --> 00:24:42,630
this infects the progenitor red cells

387
00:24:42,630 --> 00:24:44,890
and then stops erythropoiesis,

388
00:24:44,890 --> 00:24:46,690
which then results in anemia,

389
00:24:47,190 --> 00:24:49,910
predominantly when there's pre-existing marrow stress.

390
00:24:50,350 --> 00:24:50,930
So you and I,

391
00:24:51,030 --> 00:24:53,130
if we got a parvovirus B19 infection,

392
00:24:53,270 --> 00:24:55,610
would probably be relatively asymptomatic,

393
00:24:56,030 --> 00:24:57,530
but a patient who's highly dependent

394
00:24:57,530 --> 00:25:00,410
on their reserve of bone marrow

395
00:25:00,410 --> 00:25:02,270
would show signs,

396
00:25:02,490 --> 00:25:04,230
even with a minimal knockdown

397
00:25:04,230 --> 00:25:06,810
of their erythropoiesis,

398
00:25:07,030 --> 00:25:08,250
or in particular,

399
00:25:08,650 --> 00:25:10,110
their production of red blood cells.

400
00:25:10,410 --> 00:25:11,350
The treatment's supportive

401
00:25:11,350 --> 00:25:13,790
because parvovirus B19 is self-limited.

402
00:25:13,950 --> 00:25:15,530
Usually within a week to 10 days,

403
00:25:15,910 --> 00:25:17,350
the infection begins to resolve.

404
00:25:18,350 --> 00:25:20,190
Aplastic anemia is when you have damage

405
00:25:20,190 --> 00:25:21,770
to the hematopoietic stem cell.

406
00:25:21,950 --> 00:25:23,790
This results in a pancytopenia,

407
00:25:23,790 --> 00:25:25,310
i.e. low white count,

408
00:25:25,710 --> 00:25:26,710
low red blood cells,

409
00:25:26,850 --> 00:25:27,690
and low platelets,

410
00:25:28,050 --> 00:25:29,570
with a low reticulocyte count

411
00:25:29,570 --> 00:25:31,370
because you're not making red blood cells.

412
00:25:31,970 --> 00:25:33,930
Etiologies include drugs and chemicals.

413
00:25:34,150 --> 00:25:35,610
There are some drugs and chemicals

414
00:25:35,610 --> 00:25:38,710
that can classically result in aplastic anemia.

415
00:25:39,670 --> 00:25:41,530
Viral infections can also do it.

416
00:25:41,890 --> 00:25:44,110
And last cause would be autoimmune damage.

417
00:25:44,610 --> 00:25:45,950
When a biopsy is performed

418
00:25:45,950 --> 00:25:47,250
in this particular case,

419
00:25:47,330 --> 00:25:48,310
the classic finding

420
00:25:48,310 --> 00:25:50,050
is that there is an empty marrow.

421
00:25:50,510 --> 00:25:51,330
This is the bone marrow.

422
00:25:51,450 --> 00:25:52,470
Here's the spicule of bone.

423
00:25:52,810 --> 00:25:54,430
And all we see here is fat.

424
00:25:54,590 --> 00:25:55,510
Each of these circles

425
00:25:55,510 --> 00:25:57,430
represents adipose tissue or fat.

426
00:25:57,850 --> 00:25:59,010
And notice that there are basically

427
00:25:59,010 --> 00:26:00,790
no hematopoietic elements

428
00:26:00,790 --> 00:26:02,430
in this marrow space.

429
00:26:02,790 --> 00:26:04,150
So that's aplastic anemia.

430
00:26:04,670 --> 00:26:06,030
The treatment involves cessation

431
00:26:06,030 --> 00:26:07,670
of any drug that might be causing it,

432
00:26:08,150 --> 00:26:10,090
transfusions to support the patient,

433
00:26:10,530 --> 00:26:11,970
and marrow stimulating factors

434
00:26:11,970 --> 00:26:14,530
can be given in order to try

435
00:26:14,530 --> 00:26:16,470
to induce production of cells

436
00:26:16,470 --> 00:26:19,290
from whatever stem cells are present.

437
00:26:19,290 --> 00:26:21,510
So erythropoietin to drive

438
00:26:21,510 --> 00:26:22,870
the erythroid precursors

439
00:26:22,870 --> 00:26:24,010
to produce red cells.

440
00:26:25,290 --> 00:26:27,110
GMCSF and GCSF

441
00:26:27,110 --> 00:26:29,210
to drive the granulocytic precursors

442
00:26:29,210 --> 00:26:30,450
to produce granulocytes.

443
00:26:30,930 --> 00:26:32,070
Another possible treatment

444
00:26:32,070 --> 00:26:33,090
is immunosuppression.

445
00:26:33,450 --> 00:26:35,050
Recall that one of the etiologies

446
00:26:35,050 --> 00:26:37,490
of aplastic anemia is autoimmune.

447
00:26:37,910 --> 00:26:39,190
And so immunosuppression

448
00:26:39,190 --> 00:26:41,330
can potentially knock down

449
00:26:41,330 --> 00:26:42,190
that autoimmunity,

450
00:26:42,350 --> 00:26:43,970
allowing for the bone marrow to come back.

451
00:26:44,230 --> 00:26:45,370
And the final last resort

452
00:26:45,370 --> 00:26:46,050
would be to give

453
00:26:46,050 --> 00:26:47,710
a bone marrow transplant.

454
00:26:47,710 --> 00:26:49,470
The very last topic

455
00:26:49,470 --> 00:26:50,390
that I want to touch on

456
00:26:50,390 --> 00:26:51,950
is a myelophysic process.

457
00:26:52,290 --> 00:26:53,710
This is a pathologic process

458
00:26:53,710 --> 00:26:55,150
that replaces the bone marrow.

459
00:26:55,490 --> 00:26:57,250
So if there's some pathologic process

460
00:26:57,250 --> 00:26:58,650
that takes over the bone marrow,

461
00:26:58,810 --> 00:27:00,590
that would impair hematopoiesis

462
00:27:00,590 --> 00:27:01,710
because that's obviously

463
00:27:01,710 --> 00:27:03,730
where the cells are generated.

464
00:27:04,310 --> 00:27:04,910
For example,

465
00:27:05,050 --> 00:27:06,790
if the patient had metastatic cancer

466
00:27:06,790 --> 00:27:08,530
that diffusely replaces

467
00:27:08,530 --> 00:27:09,290
the bone marrow,

468
00:27:09,510 --> 00:27:11,470
that would impair hematopoiesis

469
00:27:11,470 --> 00:27:13,430
resulting in pancytopenia.

470
00:27:13,430 --> 00:27:14,630
And that is called

471
00:27:14,630 --> 00:27:16,490
a myelophysic process.

472
00:27:17,050 --> 00:27:17,670
And so with that,

473
00:27:17,750 --> 00:27:18,810
we conclude our chapter

474
00:27:18,810 --> 00:27:20,850
on red blood cell disorders.

